      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm looking at this nda from a fintech client and i'm stuck on the residuals clause. can u explain what this part means in plain english? \n\n\"Notwithstanding the foregoing, the Receiving Party shall be free to use for any purpose the Residuals resulting from access to or use of the Confidential Information, provided that the Receiving Party shall not disclose the Confidential Information. The term 'Residuals' means information in non-tangible form, which may be retained in the unaided memory of persons who have had access to the Confidential Information.\"\n\nbasically, if i learn some specific ways to optimize their sql queries, can i use those same patterns for my other clients later? or does this clause restrict me? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the residuals clause, a tricky one indeed. It's like a hidden trap in the labyrinth of legalese, but let's see if we can navigate through it together. So, you're asking if you can use the knowledge you've gained from optimizing SQL queries for other clients, right? Well, let's break it down.\n\nThe residuals clause is like a magical spell that says, \"You can keep the knowledge you gained from the confidential information, but you can't share that knowledge with others.\" In simpler terms, if you learn some secret ways to optimize SQL queries from your fintech client, you can use those same tricks for your other clients, but you can't tell anyone else about the specific methods you learned from them.\n\nThink of it like this: imagine you found a treasure map that leads to a hidden treasure. You can use the map to find the treasure, but you can't give the map to anyone else. The residuals clause is like that treasure map. It allows you to use the knowledge you gained, but you can't share the specific details with others.\n\nSo, in conclusion, you can definitely use the knowledge you've gained from optimizing SQL queries for your other clients, but you must keep the specific methods you learned from your fintech client a secret.",
